Their common angular displacement is about +0.2°, consistent with the allowed zero/specimen-height terms. I’m building the first three-phase Rietveld checkpoint now; any residual family will be assessed only after cells and line shapes are physically developed.

The three-phase model drops Rwp from ~52% to ~10.6%, strongly confirming the inventory, but the first profile pass is not physically acceptable: the common profile hit laboratory bounds and zero/height were ~99% correlated. I’m rolling back to the cell-refined checkpoint, fixing the redundant overall sample scale, and separating phase broadening from instrumental broadening before interpreting residual peaks.

The restarted profile branch is behaving more sensibly: Ba₃W₂O₉ settles near a submicron coherent size, BaCO₃ remains finite, and only WO₃ reaches the “effectively large crystallite” end of the supplied range. The current strain pass is computationally slower; I’m letting it complete before deciding whether the WO₃ size term is identifiable or should be fixed.

The Ba₃W₂O₉ strain term refined normally, but GSAS-II repeatedly stalls specifically on the monoclinic WO₃ isotropic-strain derivative. I’m treating that as numerical non-identifiability, not phase rejection: I’ll test WO₃ broadening by fixed-value profile scans and keep only a stable term if the data support it.

The profile checkpoint now has interior U, X, and axial-divergence values; only W tried to cross its lower laboratory limit, so I’m fixing W at the last interior value. A key structural correction also emerged: the raw COD files carry inconsistent/zero ADPs, while the frozen generator normalizes every reference to Uiso = 0.005 Å² before applying a per-phase value. I’m restoring that common reference convention before calibration and bounded coordinate refinement.

The coordinate tests were decisive about support: Ba/W coordinates improve the fit without hitting limits, while nearly every oxygen coordinate ran to the ±0.10 Å window. The carbonate C coordinate also hit a limit. I’ve stopped the joint pass and will retain only the stable heavy-atom shifts, rebuilding them from the clean pre-coordinate checkpoint so invalid light-atom excursions cannot contaminate the model.

The texture comparison found a clear, physically allowed winner: Ba₃W₂O₉ with March–Dollase axis [110], reducing Rwp from 9.59% to 6.26%. The [001], [100]/[010], and [111] starts were all worse, with [111] reaching its bound. WO₃ and BaCO₃ showed only marginal isolated gains, so I’ll first converge the dominant [110] branch, then retest those minor terms on the same developed checkpoint.

On the fully developed checkpoint, the minor-phase texture tests remain reproducible: WO₃ [010] improves Rwp by 0.136% and BaCO₃ [001] by 0.038%, with interior ratios. The joint three-texture/Uiso model reaches 5.71% without bound hits. I’m now testing the remaining shared intensity terms—polarization first, then a separate surface-roughness branch—so a nuisance term is retained only if it is both physical and independently beneficial.

The remaining large residuals are intensity redistribution within the WO₃ family, not unindexed peaks. The current WO₃ reference overpredicts the ~33.49° member and underpredicts ~34.40°, which is exactly where other monoclinic WO₃ references differ. Before polishing profiles, I’m doing an equal-development comparison of the exact-composition WO₃ structures: each gets the same cell window, W-coordinate window, common Uiso treatment, and all five allowed texture-axis starts.

The first alternative is decisively better: COD 2311041 lowers the equally developed WO₃ branch from 5.67% to 4.26% Rwp, with an interior Uiso and [110] texture ratio. This confirms the original WO₃ topology was a convincing positional match but the wrong intensity basin. I’m still completing the lower-symmetry members of the same exact-composition family before selecting the physical winner.

The exact-composition comparison closes the structural question. COD 2311041 is the only physical basin that both converges and removes the WO₃ intensity residuals (Rwp 4.26%); 1528915 remains at 5.67%, while 2106293/2107312 require bound-hit coordinates or ADPs and fit very poorly. The P2₁/m start failed numerically, so I treat it as unknown rather than a rejection. With every coherent family now assigned, I’m stopping phase search and finishing the 2311041 model.

The phase assemblage is closed chemically and crystallographically. I’m now resolving the remaining center/wing residuals with bounded anisotropic broadening tests, then I’ll settle the model, verify geometry and replay stability, and invoke the required one-time polish.

A consistency check found that unconstrained site-by-site displacement factors had drifted outside the laboratory model for the weak phases. The specimen generator uses one isotropic U value per phase, so I’m restoring that physical constraint before judging the profile branches.

The broadening test is decisive: Ba₃W₂O₉ needs strong isotropic size/strain broadening, while all three allowed uniaxial axes collapse to axial/equatorial ratios within about 3% and improve Rwp by only ~0.003 points. BaCO₃ strain independently refines below zero, so I’m retaining zero strain there and settling the simpler physical profile.

The common U term is the only materially supported instrumental update (29.85 → 23.40, Rwp 3.922 → 3.834, well inside bounds). W tries to cross its laboratory lower limit and is rejected; X and axial divergence are only marginal. I’m now re-equilibrating the accepted U term against phase sizes before deciding those smaller profile contributions.

The width decomposition is now physical and stable: all crystallite sizes are interior (about 0.214, 0.985, and 0.280 µm), Ba₃W₂O₉ strain is ~2274 ppm, and the other phases prefer zero strain. I’m moving to the final position calibration/cell replay, keeping zero and specimen displacement separate because they are strongly correlated.

Cell/calibration replay stayed well inside bounds and converged; the changes are small but coherent. I’m now rechecking only previously supported cation coordinates, followed by the constrained per-phase U and texture terms. Oxygen and carbonate coordinates remain fixed for the mandated polish comparison because earlier unconstrained tests drove them to bounds.

The supported coordinate replay reduced Rwp from 3.77% to 3.59%, and re-equilibrating widths/asymmetry reduced it further to 3.31%; all terms remain interior and converged. The remaining large residuals sit on accepted Ba₃W₂O₉ reflections rather than forming a new peak family. I’m doing one full replay of the supported structural/intensity/profile terms before freezing the polish input.

The replay is stable at Rwp 3.2941% (maximum shift/esd \(3\times10^{-6}\)); phase fractions and every accepted physical term are stable and interior. I’m performing the pre-polish geometry/flag audit now, then I’ll freeze `final.gpx` and run the required bounded polish exactly once.

